INT. HARGREAVE MANOR - KITCHEN - NIGHT

Black range, copper pans, a scrubbed oak table. A dumbwaiter hatch in the wall, shut. A single gaslight hisses.

MRS. CONSTANCE HARGREAVE, fifties, in black bombazine, stands at the table, her hands on its edge. The house is silent. The cook has gone to bed.

The dumbwaiter rope creaks. She freezes.

MRS. HARGREAVE
Who is hauling that?

The rope creaks again. The hatch shudders. A VOICE drifts down the shaft, thin and echoing.

VOICE (V.O.)
Constance?

Mrs. Hargreave goes white.

MRS. HARGREAVE
That isn't possible.

VOICE (V.O.)
Constance, it's cold up here.

MRS. HARGREAVE
Be quiet.

The hatch slides open an inch, on its own. Black beyond. A smell of damp earth.

The kitchen door swings in. DR. ELIOT PEMBERTON, sixties, in a travelling coat, shakes snow from his hat and stops.

PEMBERTON
Good Lord. What's happened?

Mrs. Hargreave points at the hatch with a trembling hand.

MRS. HARGREAVE
My husband is in the wall.

The gaslight gutters out.
